course: books
lesson: 29
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter II, part 1: The Open Road (the Ducks' Ditty, and a visit to Toad Hall)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: One bright summer morning the Rat has been swimming with the ducks, tickling their necks whenever they stand on their heads underwater, until they beg him to go away. So he sits on the bank and makes up a little song about them, «Ducks' Ditty».
explain: The Mole, who is honest and no poet, says he doesn't think much of it. The ducks don't either, laughs the Rat — they say it's nonsense to sit on banks making poems about other people. Then the Mole asks the favour he's been waiting to ask: will the Rat take him to meet Mr. Toad?
explain: Of course! says the Rat. Toad is always glad to see you and always sorry when you go. He's kind and good-natured — though perhaps not very clever, and rather boastful and conceited.
explain: Round a bend they see Toad Hall: a grand old red-brick house with lawns down to the water. Toad is rich, the Rat says, and it's one of the nicest houses around — though they never tell Toad that.
explain: In the big boat-house all the fine boats hang up unused, and the place feels deserted. The Rat understands at once: Toad is done with boating. What is his new craze? They go to find out.
fact: The Rat had been «swimming in the river, in company with his friends the ducks»; when the ducks stood on their heads he would «dive down and tickle their necks, just under where their chins would be if ducks had chins».
fact: «it is impossible to say quite all you feel when your head is under water» — so the ducks came up «spluttering and angry» and told him to mind his own business.
fact: The song «Ducks' Ditty» begins: «All along the backwater, Through the rushes tall, Ducks are a-dabbling, Up tails all!» (a ditty = a short simple song; dabbling = dipping heads into water to feed).
fact: More of the song: «Slushy green undergrowth Where the roach swim— Here we keep our larder, Cool and full and dim.» (roach = a small river fish; larder = food store) and «Everyone for what he likes! We like to be Heads down, tails up, Dabbling free!»
fact: The last verse: «High in the blue above Swifts whirl and call— We are down a-dabbling Uptails all!» So «Up tails all!» comes twice: at the end of the first verse and as the very last line.
fact: The Mole «was no poet himself and didn't care who knew it; and he had a candid nature» (candid = honest, saying what he thinks).
fact: The Rat says the ducks ask why fellows can't «do what they like when they like and as they like, instead of other fellows sitting on banks and watching them all the time and making remarks and poetry».
fact: The Mole agrees «So it is, so it is» — «No, it isn't!» cries the Rat — «Well then, it isn't, it isn't,» says the Mole soothingly: he changes his mind at once to keep the peace.
fact: The Rat on Toad: «Always good-tempered, always glad to see you, always sorry when you go!» and «So simple, so good-natured, and so affectionate. Perhaps he's not very clever ... and it may be that he is both boastful and conceited.»
fact: The Rat says any time is a good time to call on Toad.
fact: Conceited = thinks too much of himself; boastful = always showing off about what he has or does.
fact: Toad Hall is «a handsome, dignified old house of mellowed red brick, with well-kept lawns reaching down to the water's edge».
fact: A notice-board says «Private. No landing allowed»; there are stables and a very old banqueting-hall (a big dining hall for feasts).
fact: «Toad is rather rich, you know, and this is really one of the nicest houses in these parts, though we never admit as much to Toad.»
fact: In the boat-house the boats were «slung from the cross beams or hauled up on a slip, but none in the water» (the Mole, who can row now, had shipped his sculls as they glided in); «Boating is played out. He's tired of it, and done with it. I wonder what new fad he has taken up now?» (a fad = a short-lived craze).
fact: Old words: «implored» = begged; «heartiness» = warm eagerness; «make his acquaintance» = meet him; «mellowed» = softened and warm-coloured with age; «shipped his sculls» = pulled the oars into the boat.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «it's giving» = it looks like, it feels like; «wicked» (New England) = very; «heaps» (Australia, NZ) = lots; «bostin'» (Black Country) = brilliant; «lekker» (South Africa) = great; «rad» (old US) = cool.
exercise: Who had the Rat been swimming with since early morning? | The ducks | The Otter and his young son | The sleepy Mole | The moorhens
exercise: What did the Rat do when the ducks stood on their heads? | Tickled their necks | Pulled their tails up and down | Pushed them over into the reeds | Sang to them underwater
exercise: Why were the ducks angry? | The Rat kept tickling them underwater | The Rat ate their food and drank their water | The Mole teased them about their tails | It rained
exercise: Why could the ducks not tell the Rat everything they felt? | You can't say much with your head underwater | They are very shy, polite birds who never complain | They were laughing | They don't speak
exercise: What is the Rat's song called? | «Ducks' Ditty» | «Messing About in Boats» | «The Wind in the Willows» | «Up We Go, Ducks»
exercise: «Ducks are a-dabbling, Up tails all!» What is dabbling? | Dipping heads in the water to feed | Splashing about angrily with their wings at rats | Flying | Sleeping
exercise: Where do the ducks «keep our larder» in the song? | In the slushy green undergrowth where the roach swim | High in the blue, where the swifts whirl | In the Rat's hole | On the bank
exercise: «High in the blue above / Swifts whirl and call». What are swifts? | Birds | Flying fish | Clouds | Sailing boats
exercise: What does the Mole say about the song? | He doesn't think very much of it | It's the best song he has ever heard in his whole life | He wants to learn it | Nothing
exercise: «he had a candid nature». What does «candid» mean? | Honest and open | Sweet like sugar candy, and kind to everyone he meets | Shy | Clever
exercise: What do the ducks think of the Rat's poem, he says? | They think it's nonsense | They love it and sing it every morning | They never heard it | They want another
exercise: What favour does the Mole ask? | To be taken to meet Mr. Toad | To be taught to write poems as good as the Rat's | To go to the Wild Wood | To row
exercise: According to the Rat, when is the wrong time to call on Toad? | Never | Early in the morning | At dinner-time | In winter
exercise: Which is NOT one of the Rat's words about Toad? | Shy and modest | Very good-natured | Very affectionate | Boastful and conceited
exercise: «he is both boastful and conceited». What does «conceited» mean? | Thinking far too much of yourself | Very kind to others | Very clever with money | Lazy
exercise: What is Toad Hall built of? | Mellowed red brick | Grey stone from the quarry beyond the Wild Wood | Wood | Mud
exercise: What does the notice-board on the creek say? | «Private. No landing allowed» | «Welcome, all friends of Toad» | «Sixpence to pass» | «Beware of the dog»
exercise: What is very old at Toad Hall, the Rat says? | The banqueting-hall | The boat-house | The caravan | The lawns
exercise: Is Toad rich or poor? | Rather rich | Very poor, but proud | Neither rich nor poor | Nobody knows
exercise: What do the Rat and his friends never admit to Toad? | That his house is one of the nicest around | That they don't like him | That they can't row | That he is rich
exercise: What do they see in the boat-house? | Many fine boats, but none in the water | Toad, polishing his new wager-boat for a race | Nothing at all | A caravan
exercise: What does the Rat decide from the empty boat-house? | Toad has got tired of boating | Toad has drowned | The boats were stolen | Toad is away
exercise: «I wonder what new fad he has taken up now?» What is a fad? | A short-lived craze | A long holiday by the seaside | A kind of boat | A job
test: Tom, wicked (very) sure of it: «the Rat's song was about the Mole falling in the river.» Put it right. | It was about ducks dabbling | Right: it was all about the Mole's big Sploosh and the Rat's rescue of the luncheon-basket | It was about Toad | About the Wild Wood
test: «Nor don't the ducks neither». What is odd about the Rat's words? | Two «no» words in a row — chatty, not school grammar | He is speaking a secret duck language that only rats understand | It's French | Nothing
test: «So it is, so it is» — «No, it isn't!» — «Well then, it isn't, it isn't». Why does the Mole flip like this? | To keep the peace and get on to his favour | He really changes his mind each time after thinking it over very carefully | He's deaf | He's teasing the ducks
test: The ducks ask why «fellows» can't be left to do what they like without others watching and «making remarks and poetry». Is that fair? | Yes, a bit: nobody likes being watched and written about | No: ducks love poems | No: ducks can't think | Yes: poems are always rude
test: Jess, bostin' (brilliant) mood: «the Rat said Toad's a genius and dead modest». Put it right. | «Perhaps he's not very clever», and boastful | Right: Toad is the cleverest, most modest animal on the whole river, and everyone says so | Toad's shy | Toad's mean
test: «Always glad to see you, always sorry when you go!» What kind of host is Toad? | Friendly and welcoming | Grumpy, like Badger | Shy | Never at home
test: Why do they «never admit» to Toad that his house is lovely? | He's boastful enough already | Toad would charge them sixpence to come in if he knew | They don't like it | It isn't true
test: Looking back: in chapter I the Otter said Toad's craze was what? | A brand-new racing boat | A motor-car | A caravan | Punting with a long pole all day and every day on the main river
test: Looking back: in chapter I, who rowed — and now in chapter II, who takes the sculls? | The Rat then; now the Mole, who has learnt | The Rat both times, as the Mole still can't row at all | The Mole both times | The Otter
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat promise the wet Mole? | To teach him to row and swim | A new black velvet smoking-suit | A trip to Toad Hall | A boat of his own
test: Looking back: in chapter I, the Otter said Toad has «no stability». How does the empty boat-house prove it? | He's already given up boating | The boats have all sunk | The boat-house fell down | It doesn't
test: What would the Rat have done if the boat-house had been full of boats in the water? | Thought Toad was still boating | Gone straight home without a word, in a great hurry | Stolen one | Painted one
test: «a pre-occupied expression» — Toad is about to appear. Looking at the empty boat-house, what does the Rat expect? | A new craze | Toad in tears | Toad gone away | A big party
test: Which word means «honest» in this part? | candid | conceited | mellowed | boastful
test: ★★★ «just under where their chins would be if ducks had chins». What is the joke? | Ducks don't have chins, so the Rat tickles a place that doesn't exist | Ducks have very big chins | The Rat has no chin | The Mole has two chins
test: ★★★ How many times do the words «Up tails all!» (or «Uptails all!») appear in the whole song? | 2: near the start and as the very last line | 4: once at the end of every single verse | 1 | 3
test: ★★★ The Rat calls Toad «not very clever» and «conceited» — and then says «he has got some great qualities». What does this show about the Rat? | He sees Toad's faults and still likes him | He secretly hates Toad and says nice things about him only so the Mole won't guess | He's confused | He's never met Toad
exam: Identify the subject of the composition entitled «Ducks' Ditty». | Ducks dabbling in the backwater | The perils of the Wild Wood | The merits of boating | The residence of Mr. Toad
exam: Ascertain the request which the Mole addressed to the Rat on the morning in question. | That he be taken to call upon Mr. Toad | That he be taught the composition of verse | That he be conveyed to the Wild Wood | That he be permitted to dive
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises Toad Hall as described by the Rat. | One of the nicest houses in those parts, though this is never admitted to its owner | A modest dwelling in a bank | A ruin | A house-boat
exam: It is given that the boats were found unused. State the inference drawn therefrom by the Rat. | That boating was «played out» and Toad had taken up a new fad | That Toad had departed for the Wide World | That the boats were unsafe | That Toad was asleep
